# Ask for device and entity identifiers

Let people choose a device by name and room. Ask for an identifier only when a readable name cannot pick out the exact entity, and offer the device picker as an equal route.

These examples are local previews with invented device names. They do not change the registry or call a service.

## When to ask for an ID

Ask for an entity ID to import a saved configuration, to troubleshoot one entity, or to tell apart devices with identical names. For everyday control, use a labelled device picker. An ID is not proof of identity or permission.

<%= example('nhs-identifier-route') %>

## Ask for the right kind of identifier

A Home Assistant entity ID has a domain and an object name, such as `light.example_reading_lamp`. It can be renamed. An integration’s unique ID and a device registry ID mean something different and are not interchangeable with an entity ID. Name the field you want exactly. If you store a registry reference, look up the current entity each time you use it.

Accept pasted and typed text. Validate against the entities the person may use and the domains the task supports. Do not apply digit grouping, length limits or checksums. Keep what they entered after an error. Do not strip punctuation or quietly correct a near match.

<%= example('nhs-identifier-error') %>

## Confirm the match before saving

Show the friendly name, area and exact reference, and the integration when it helps. Tell “not found”, “unavailable” and “not allowed” apart, and do not reveal entities the person cannot use. An unavailable entity can still be the right one. Offer Retry or a deliberate replacement and never overwrite it automatically.

<%= example('nhs-identifier-match') %>

## Write it so everyone can use it

Use a visible label, a short example and a linked hint and error. Let long IDs wrap without changing the value people copy. Do not rely on monospace type to explain meaning. Say “Choose a device instead”, not “Invalid object”. Keyboard and screen reader users must reach that route without retyping earlier answers.

## Respect Home Assistant behaviour

These previews show choosing, not changing the registry or calling a service. A real picker must list only the entities the person may use and the capabilities they support. Keep access tokens, serial numbers and account details out of examples, messages and logs. Check the chosen entity again before saving or acting on it.
